Released issue metadata:
Issue title: GDPR Compliance?
Project: kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme
Issue type: issue
Matched keywords: "['cookie banner']"
GitHub labels: ["Type: Enhancement"]
Status in release: closed
Comments in release: 11; discussants: 2
Reported: 2018-05-18 10:39:59+00; last active: 2018-05-24 17:36:31+00
Issue number: 62

Supplemental evidence: complete | comments retrieved 11/11
Scope: Current retrievable issue body and issue comments; not the original study's historical snapshot. Linked pages and attachments are not fetched.

Issue body:
On May 25th, GDPR will come into effect and it will require any EU website/organization and non-EU with EU visitors/clients to comply with its specifics(opt-in regarding the use of cookies). While I'm not entirely sure about what this means for the theme(where are cookies used?), it would be nice to see a theme-level compliance to avoid issues on the webmasters.

Issue discussion:
Comment 1 | created 2018-05-18T18:17:46Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390290488
I don't know where cookies are used in this theme, but I think the first easily solvable issue should be adding an option to Google Analytics to anonymize IPs @kitian616 

Comment 2 | created 2018-05-20T16:38:42Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390494745
So in order for a website to be GDPR compliant, aside from merging #63 there isn't really anything which should be done I the project itself. However I have a question(two actually). According to https://cookieconsent.insites.com (which I used for my banner) you need to add the banner before </head> and I did so in the base.html layout, however nothing showed up. What am I doing wrong? Another thing is that GDPR requires cookie opt-ins and the possibility to revoke the consent, which means first load should disable all cookies, the above mentioned website gives a code block [here](https://cookieconsent.insites.com/documentation/disabling-cookies/) but I have no idea where I should put it. Ideas?

Comment 3 | created 2018-05-21T11:28:45Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390628356
Add code like this?
```html
<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="//cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/cookieconsent2/3.0.3/cookieconsent.min.css" />
<script src="//cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/cookieconsent2/3.0.3/cookieconsent.min.js"></script>
<script>
window.addEventListener("load", function(){
  ...
});
</script>
```
If so, maybe  because the `load` event emitted before you use `addEventListener` to bind the handler function.

Comment 4 | created 2018-05-21T11:31:10Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390628839
Yea I managed to get the banner working, however for callback hook to actually block cookies, I have no clue. Any ideas?

Comment 5 | created 2018-05-21T11:40:59Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390630656
You can just try to remove the addEventListener if you put this code at the bottom of the `<body>` block, eg:
```html
(function() {
  ...
})();
```

And also, you can use `window.pageLoad` to handle the callback, even the `load` event has been emitted, the callback would still working:
```
window.pageLoad.then(function() {
  ...
});
```
You can see the source [here](https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/blob/master/_includes/scripts/utils.js#L17)

Comment 6 | created 2018-05-21T12:29:58Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390640272
So basically I should put this inside utils.js?
```onInitialise: function (status) {
  var type = this.options.type;
  var didConsent = this.hasConsented();
  if (type == 'opt-in' && didConsent) {
    // enable cookies
  }
  if (type == 'opt-out' && !didConsent) {
    // disable cookies
  }
},
 
onStatusChange: function(status, chosenBefore) {
  var type = this.options.type;
  var didConsent = this.hasConsented();
  if (type == 'opt-in' && didConsent) {
    // enable cookies
  }
  if (type == 'opt-out' && !didConsent) {
    // disable cookies
  }
},
 
onRevokeChoice: function() {
  var type = this.options.type;
  if (type == 'opt-in') {
    // disable cookies
  }
  if (type == 'opt-out') {
    // enable cookies
  }
},
```
Like I said I've no clue on this.


Comment 7 | created 2018-05-22T03:22:16Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390850692
You can put it in utils.js, and you can also put it in common. https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/blob/master/_includes/scripts/common.js

Comment 8 | created 2018-05-22T06:19:22Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-390875772
Mmm, how do I specify Disqus cookie? The only one that needs prior consent is Disqus if you set IP anonymization in google analytics.

Comment 9 | created 2018-05-24T07:22:20Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-391615228
Fixed the problem thank you! So long as #63 (the one in the comment tho) is merged this issue can be closed as it's the only one modification that truly needs to be added to the code itself.

Comment 10 | created 2018-05-24T09:44:31Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-391654706
errr.. why you want to specify Disqus cookie,  something to do with the banner?

so it's almost May 25th, I'll merge it tonight.

Comment 11 | created 2018-05-24T09:46:11Z
Source: https://github.com/kitian616/jekyll-TeXt-theme/issues/62#issuecomment-391655206
Well you need to disable Disqus entirely until the users click "Agree" on the banner, and you also need to disable Disqus once again if the users ever revoke their choice. Welcome to EU Laws

